%S A346072 3,16,21,36,45,57,69,77,95,99,100,133,139,141,185,217,247,271,349,812,
%T A346072 834,882,884,1012,1018,1078,1138,1198,1256,1404,1478,1936,1985,2263,
%U A346072 2345,2381,2477,2489,2549,2583,2631,2847,2855,2857,2865,2887,2903,2947,2969,2977,3005,3011,3023,3028
%N A346072 Numbers m such that there exist positive integers i < m and j > m such that m = Sum_{k=i..m-1} tau(k) and m = Sum_{k=m+1..j} tau(k), where tau(k) = number of divisors of k = A000005(k).
%C A346072 The terms show a wavelike growth pattern as n increases. See the linked image.
%C A346072 The wavelike structure consists of clusters of terms separated by large jumps, e.g. a(19) = 349 jumps to a(20) = 812. Each run's sum (over i..m-1 and over m+1..j) has the same parity as the number of squares it contains. tau(k) is odd if and only if k is a square, otherwise it is even. Adding an even tau leaves the parity unchanged and adding an odd tau (at a square) flips it. If m is a term, the two runs together contain an even number of squares (since both sums equal m, they have the same parity). The total number of squares in the two runs grows slowly with m. Where it is mostly odd, terms are rare, and those stretches are the jumps. The total is about sqrt(m)/(log(m) + 2*gamma) where gamma is Euler's constant (A001620) because tau averages about log(m) + 2*gamma near m. Jumps fall near m where this is an odd integer, e.g. about 3 near m = 474. The total grows ever more slowly, so the waves get longer. For m <= 2.55*10^7, the rate of terms near every odd value from 3 to 275 was lower than near both neighboring even values, about 11 times lower on average. - _Jennifer Voitle_, Nov 10 2026
%H A346072 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A346072/b346072.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..5000</a>.
%H A346072 Scott R. Shannon, <a href="/A346072/a346072.png">Image of the first 5000 terms</a>.
%H A346072 Jennifer Voitle, <a href="/A346072/a346072.py.txt">Python program</a> (computes terms and the even-tau control).
%e A346072 3 is a term as tau(1) + tau(2) = 1 + 2 = 3, and tau(4) = 3.
%e A346072 16 is a term as tau(12) + tau(13) + tau(14) + tau(15) = 6 + 2 + 4 + 4 = 16 and tau(17) + tau(18) + tau(19) + tau(20) = 2 + 6 + 2 + 6 = 16.
%e A346072 21 is a term as tau(16) + ... + tau(20) = 5 + 2 + 6 + 2 + 6 = 21 and tau(22) + ... tau(26) = 4 + 2 + 8 + 3 + 4 = 21.
%e A346072 Consider m=500, which lies in the gap between a(19) =349 and a(20) = 812. The run tau(432) + ... + tau(499) sums to 500 and contains two squares, 441 and 484. The run starting at 501 contains only one square, 529, so its totals go from 495 to 503, skipping over 500. The upper run's total is even until it passes 529 which is a square, then becomes odd. The run stays odd until 576, which comes after the total has already passed 500. The parity of the lower run is even (contains two squares) while the parity of the upper run is odd (a single square). So m = 500 is not a term. - _Jennifer Voitle_, Nov 10 2026
